<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-statement">
  <title>The classical DFP question and its negative answer</title>
  <p>
    Here and below, vector norms are Euclidean, matrix norms are spectral, and <m>I</m>
denotes the identity matrix of the appropriate size.  For symmetric matrices
<m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, the notation <m>A\preceq B</m> means that <m>B-A</m> is positive
semidefinite, and <m>A\succ0</m> means that <m>A</m> is positive definite.  Let
<m>x_k\in\R^n</m> be the <m>k</m>th iterate, let <m>g_k=\nabla f(x_k)</m>, let <m>H_k\succ0</m> be
the current inverse Hessian approximation, and let <m>\alpha_k>0</m> be the
accepted step length.  The classical DFP method is
    <md>
      <mrow>d_k&amp;=-H_kg_k,&amp;s_k&amp;=\alpha_kd_k,&amp;x_{k+1}&amp;=x_k+s_k,</mrow>
      <mrow>y_k&amp;=g_{k+1}-g_k,&amp;
H_{k+1}&amp;=H_k-
\frac{H_ky_ky_k^TH_k}{y_k^TH_ky_k}
+\frac{s_ks_k^T}{s_k^Ty_k}.</mrow>
    </md>
    Thus <m>d_k</m> is the search direction, <m>s_k</m> is the accepted step, and
<m>y_k</m> is the gradient difference used in the update.
  </p>
  <p>
    The standard weak Wolfe conditions <xref ref="bib-Wolfe1969"/> are
    <mdn>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-armijo">f(x_{k+1})&amp;\le f(x_k)+c_1g_k^Ts_k,</mrow>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-weak-curvature">g_{k+1}^Ts_k&amp;\ge c_2g_k^Ts_k,</mrow>
    </mdn>
    where <m>0&lt;c_1&lt;c_2&lt;1</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The standard strong Wolfe conditions use <xref ref="eq-armijo"/> and replace
<xref ref="eq-weak-curvature"/> by
    <men xml:id="eq-strong-curvature">|g_{k+1}^Ts_k|\le c_2|g_k^Ts_k|.</men>
  </p>
  <p>
    In this paper, a line search accepts a step when its positive step length
satisfies the stated inequalities at <m>x_k</m> and <m>x_{k+1}</m>.  We do not tie this
definition to a particular bracketing, interpolation, or zoom procedure.  If
<m>H_k\succ0</m> and <m>g_k\ne0</m>, then
<m>g_k^Td_k&lt;0</m>.  Either curvature condition
implies
<me>s_k^Ty_k=(g_{k+1}-g_k)^Ts_k
\ge (c_2-1)g_k^Ts_k>0,</me>
so the DFP denominators are positive and <m>H_{k+1}\succ0</m>.  In our
construction, positivity also follows directly from the prescribed secant
pairs.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classical open question is the following.  Suppose
<m>f\in C^2(\R^n)</m> satisfies
<m>mI\preceq\nabla^2f(x)\preceq MI</m> on the relevant level set for constants
<m>0&lt;m\le M&lt;\infty</m>, let <m>H_0\succ0</m> be arbitrary, and let every accepted
positive step satisfy <xref ref="eq-armijo"/><ndash/><xref ref="eq-weak-curvature"/>.  Must the
unmodified DFP iterates satisfy
    <men xml:id="eq-open-question">\|\nabla f(x_k)\|\longrightarrow0</men>
    for every sequence of step lengths that satisfies these conditions?  A
uniformly convex objective has a unique minimizer, so
<xref ref="eq-open-question"/> is equivalent to
convergence of the iterates to that minimizer.  The statement covers every
initial inverse Hessian approximation and every accepted weak Wolfe step.  A
counterexample needs only one uniformly convex objective, one positive
definite initial matrix, and one infinite sequence of valid steps for which
<xref ref="eq-open-question"/> fails.
  </p>
  <p>
    The objective in our counterexample is a locally supported perturbation of a
quadratic.  The discrete data <m>(x_k,g_k,H_k,\alpha_k)</m> are constructed in
Sections<nbsp/><xref ref="sec-alternating-sequence" text="global"/><ndash/><xref ref="sec-global-objective" text="global"/>.  They
satisfy the DFP recurrence before the objective is introduced.  In the formula
below, <m>x\in\R^2</m> is an arbitrary point, whereas <m>x_k</m> is the <m>k</m>th iterate.
Let <m>C_\infty=\lim_{k\to\infty}(x_k-g_k)</m>, and let <m>\rho_k>0</m> be the support
radii defined in <xref ref="eq-rho-k"/>.  We write
<m>\mathbb B(c,r)=\{u\in\R^2:\|u-c\|&lt;r\}</m> and fix a smooth function
<m>\chi:\R^2\to[0,1]</m> supported in <m>\mathbb B(0,1)</m> such that <m>\chi=1</m> on
<m>\mathbb B(0,1/3)</m>.  The final objective has the explicit form
    <men xml:id="eq-objective">\begin{aligned}
f(x)&amp;=\frac12\|x-C_\infty\|^2
+\sum_{k=0}^\infty
\chi\!\left(\frac{x-x_k}{\rho_k}\right)a_k^T(x-x_k),\\
a_k&amp;=g_k-(x_k-C_\infty).
\end{aligned}</men>
  </p>
  <p>
    The radii are chosen so that the supports in this sum are pairwise disjoint.
At every prescribed point, the construction gives
<me>f(x_k)=\frac12\|x_k-C_\infty\|^2,\qquad
\nabla f(x_k)=g_k,\qquad
\nabla^2f(x_k)=I.</me>
Thus the sequence is used only to specify interpolation data.  Once all the
data have been fixed, <xref ref="eq-objective"/> defines one function on <m>\R^2</m>;
the function is not changed during the DFP iteration.  The later sections
prove that the series defines a globally <m>C^2</m> uniformly convex function and
that classical DFP with the prescribed step lengths reproduces the sequence.
The main theorem records the result.  Its accepted steps even satisfy the
standard strong Wolfe conditions.
  </p>
  <theorem xml:id="thm-main">
    <title>Main theorem</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For every pair of constants satisfying
        <men xml:id="eq-wolfe-range">0&lt;c_1&lt;\frac23,\qquad \frac23\le c_2&lt;1,</men>
        there exist <m>f\in C^2(\R^2)</m>, <m>x_0\in\R^2</m>, <m>H_0\succ0</m>, and positive step
lengths <m>\alpha_k</m> such that
<me>\frac12I\preceq\nabla^2f(x)\preceq\frac32I
\qquad(x\in\R^2),</me>
all classical DFP iterates are well defined, every step satisfies
<xref ref="eq-armijo"/> and <xref ref="eq-strong-curvature"/> with these constants, but
there is a constant <m>G_\infty>0</m> such that
<me>\|\nabla f(x_k)\|\longrightarrow G_\infty.</me>
The same conclusion holds in every dimension <m>n\ge2</m> by an orthogonal direct
sum.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="DFPWolfe" ref="DFP.existsStrongWolfeCounterexample_of_parameterRange">Lean (existsStrongWolfeCounterexample_of_parameterRange)</lean>
    <lean project="DFPWolfe" ref="DFP.main_not_globalWeakWolfeConvergence_of_parameterRange">Lean (main_not_globalWeakWolfeConvergence_of_parameterRange)</lean>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Section<nbsp/><xref ref="sec-alternating-sequence" text="global"/> constructs an infinite
two-dimensional sequence satisfying the classical DFP search-direction, secant, and update identities.  It also proves that the gradient norms converge to a positive constant.  Section<nbsp/><xref ref="sec-global-objective" text="global"/> establishes a uniform separation bound and realizes the prescribed endpoint
data by a globally <m>C^2</m> function satisfying
<m>\frac12 I\preceq\nabla^2 f\preceq\frac32 I</m>.
Section<nbsp/><xref ref="sec-completion" text="global"/> verifies the Armijo and standard strong Wolfe conditions and extends the construction to every dimension <m>n\ge2</m>.
The detailed arguments are given in those sections.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>
  <p>
    Equivalently, for every coefficient pair in <xref ref="eq-wolfe-range"/>, the
fixed-coefficient global weak-Wolfe convergence predicate is false.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Hessian bounds in Theorem<nbsp/><xref ref="thm-main" text="global"/> give a global condition number of
at most three.  The threshold <m>c_2=2/3</m> is exact for this construction: on the
two iterations of each cycle, the ratios
<m>|g_{k+1}^Ts_k|/|g_k^Ts_k|</m> equal <m>1/3</m> and <m>2/3</m>.  The restriction <m>c_1&lt;2/3</m>
comes from the smaller of the two limiting normalized objective decreases in
the Armijo condition.  The main theorem does not require the standard
initialization <m>H_0=I</m>.  This raises a natural question: does the
nonconvergence depend on the use of a nonidentity initial matrix?
The affine normalization in the following corollary shows that it does not.
  </p>
  <corollary xml:id="cor-identity-initialization">
    <title>Identity initialization</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Fix any pair <m>(c_1,c_2)</m> satisfying <xref ref="eq-wolfe-range"/>.  There exist
<m>\widetilde f\in C^2(\R^2)</m>, <m>\widetilde x_0\in\R^2</m>, positive step lengths <m>\alpha_k</m>,
and constants <m>0&lt;\widetilde m\le \widetilde M&lt;\infty</m> such that
<me>\widetilde m I\preceq\nabla^2\widetilde f(x)
\preceq\widetilde M I\qquad(x\in\R^2).</me>
For this objective, classical DFP initialized with <m>\widetilde H_0=I</m> is well
defined.  Every accepted step satisfies <xref ref="eq-armijo"/> and
<xref ref="eq-strong-curvature"/>, but
<me>\liminf_{k\to\infty}\|\nabla\widetilde f(\widetilde x_k)\|>0.</me>
The same conclusion holds in every dimension <m>n\ge2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <lean project="DFPWolfe" ref="DFP.existsMatrixIdentityLiminfStrongWolfe_of_parameterRange">Lean</lean>
  </corollary>
  <p>
    Corollary<nbsp/><xref ref="cor-identity-initialization" text="global"/> gives identity initialization, but
its Hessian bounds depend on the problem.  It does not claim that <m>H_0=I</m> and
the fixed Hessian bounds <m>[\frac12 I,\frac32 I]</m> hold at the same time.  The
proof uses an affine change of variables and appears in
Section<nbsp/><xref ref="sec-completion" text="global"/>, after the two-dimensional example is complete.
  </p>
</section>
